<TopicPage slug="subjects/logistics-supply-chain/foundational/supply-chain-concepts-and-metrics">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Logistics and Supply Chain Management, and it assumes nothing —
    no economics, no spreadsheets beyond multiplication and division. It builds the discipline's
    core mental models and its standard vocabulary of metrics, on which every later lesson
    (forecasting, inventory, sourcing, logistics) depends.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>define a supply chain and explain why coordinating one is hard even though no one owns it;</li>
    <li>trace the three flows — product, money, information — and read failures as flow problems;</li>
    <li>explain the bullwhip effect: its mechanism, causes and fixes;</li>
    <li>use the trade-off triangle (service, inventory, cost) to reason about any chain decision, and match chain type to product type;</li>
    <li>speak SCOR: the Plan–Source–Make–Deliver–Return–Enable vocabulary and the five metric families, including perfect order, inventory turns and cash-to-cash cycle time;</li>
    <li>segment customers and suppliers so that scarce service and management effort go where they pay.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit throughout: when something goes wrong in a chain, resist blaming the nearest
    company. Ask which <em>flow</em> failed, and which tier created the distortion.
  </p>

  <h2>A chain nobody owns</h2>
  <p>
    Take the phone in your pocket. Its materials came from mines on three continents; its silicon
    was refined, designed and fabricated by different specialist firms; its parts were made by
    hundreds of suppliers; it was assembled somewhere else entirely, shipped through ports and
    distribution centres, and finally sold to you by a retailer. Every one of those companies has
    its own owners, its own accounting and its own incentives — and not one of them owns the
    whole path. That path is a <strong>supply chain</strong>: every company and process standing
    between raw material in the ground and a product in a customer's hands.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Supply chain management</strong> is the discipline of coordinating all of it — which
    is genuinely strange, because no one owns the chain. Your phone's assembler does not own its
    chip supplier, which does not own the silicon refiner, which does not own the retailer. The
    field's whole problem is cooperation across boundaries that no contract can fully bridge.
  </p>
  <p>
    The classic way to see through the complexity is to stop looking at the boxes (companies,
    factories, warehouses) and look at the <strong>flows</strong> connecting them. Three of them
    run through every chain. <em>Product</em> flows downstream: ore becomes steel becomes parts
    becomes product becomes delivery — and, when things come back, a <em>reverse</em> flow runs
    upstream again (the returns that now define e-commerce). <em>Money</em> flows upstream: the
    customer's payment is split into everyone's revenue on the way back. <em>Information</em>
    flows both ways — orders and forecasts downstream-to-upstream; availability, lead times and
    shipment status upstream-to-downstream. Nearly every famous supply chain failure turns out to
    be a flow problem, not a box problem: information that was late, distorted or absent, causing
    product to pile up in the wrong place or vanish from the right one. The empty toilet-paper
    shelves of 2020 were not a shortage of pulp; they were an information cascade, as every link
    in the chain simultaneously ordered extra against the same rumour of panic buying.
  </p>
  <p>
    A second clarifying lens: the chain has only one <strong>customer</strong>. Every upstream
    participant — however many invoices it sends — ultimately serves the person who buys the
    finished product, and final demand is the only true signal. Everything else upstream is
    derived from it. That is why the discipline says the chain exists <em>downstream</em>, and why
    the hardest coordination problems start with the fact that most companies cannot see final
    demand at all: the semiconductor plant sees orders from assemblers, who see orders from
    retailers, who see actual shoppers — and each layer in between injects its own noise.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="flows, trade-offs and the SCOR vocabulary" widget="equationStep" config={chainCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the five steps are this lesson's map. Steps 1 and 2 are the
    two lenses you have just read; step 3 (the bullwhip) is what happens when the information
    flow distorts; step 4 (the trade-off triangle) is the constraint every decision bends inside;
    and step 5 (SCOR) is the vocabulary the rest of the industry will expect you to speak. Keep
    this panel in mind as a table of contents — each step gets its own section below.
  </p>

  <h2>The end-to-end chain: trade-offs and the bullwhip</h2>
  <p>
    Managed well, the chain is invisible — you click, the item arrives. Managed badly it is empty
    shelves, idled factories, expediting fees and a very loud news cycle. The design tension that
    governs everything is the <strong>trade-off triangle</strong>: <em>service level</em> (how
    reliably demand is met, on time and in full), <em>inventory</em> (how much capital and space
    is tied up in stock) and <em>cost</em> (the total expense of sourcing, making and moving). You
    can perform brilliantly on any two; the third collects the bill. A chain can promise
    near-perfect availability with minimal inventory only by paying for speed — air freight,
    duplicate suppliers, buffer capacity. Every decision from network design to the size of a
    purchase order lives inside this triangle, and most supply chain arguments are really
    arguments about which corner to sacrifice.
  </p>
  <p>
    Underneath the flows sits a strategic choice that Marshall Fisher (1997) made canonical:
    match the chain to the product. <strong>Functional products</strong> — detergent, staple
    foods, commodity parts — have predictable demand and compete on price; they deserve
    <em>efficient</em> chains that squeeze cost, run full trucks and hold lean inventory.
    <strong>Innovative products</strong> — fashion, electronics, anything with a short season and
    volatile demand — deserve <em>responsive</em> chains that buy speed and flexibility even at
    higher cost, because the expensive failure is not the air freight bill but the markdown bin
    full of last season's stock. Mismatched pairs are where supply chains visibly fail: the
    efficient chain serving fashion produces chronic stockouts of the hits and mountains of the
    misses; the responsive chain serving detergent simply costs more than the competition can
    match.
  </p>
  <p>
    The choice is made deliberately at every scale. One small online retailer of customised
    wedding stationery shows the machinery: sell direct to consumers (channel structure — the
    nervous bride gets one accountable point of contact), buy designs from a freelance network
    rather than employing designers (sourcing), keep order-confirmation and proofing in-house but
    hand printing to one carefully chosen local print partner (manufacturing), use a single
    national shipper with tracking integrated into its own customer database (delivery), and let
    the same team that owns each relationship also review its performance (issue resolution). Six
    decisions, each trading a little of one corner of the triangle for another — an efficient
    chain would be cheaper, but responsiveness to the individual bride <em>is</em> the product.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a chain is flows, not boxes; it serves one final customer; every
    design decision trades service, inventory and cost. Now the most famous demonstration of why
    coordination is hard anyway.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>bullwhip effect</strong> works like this. A retailer sees shopper demand wiggle
    slightly — say, a case of soup selling unusually well one week. Uncertain whether it is a
    trend, and unwilling to lose sales, the retailer orders up, with a buffer. The wholesaler sees
    a bigger order jump and, applying its own safety margin, orders up harder from the factory.
    The factory sees what looks like a boom, adds capacity and raw material orders, and the
    suppliers of suppliers amplify again. Small wobbles at the shelf become violent swings
    upstream — the crack of a whip — so factories and mines live through booms and busts that the
    actual shopper never caused. It was first formalised from Procter &amp; Gamble's observation
    that diaper orders swung wildly even though babies consume diapers at an almost perfectly
    steady rate.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="each stage orders more wildly than the last" widget="plotter" config={bullwhipCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the curve steepens left to right — each tier does not merely
    pass on the variability it received, it <em>multiplies</em> it. Stage 1 is the retailer whose
    orders wiggle; stage 5 is raw-material supply, living through swings several times larger than
    anything the shopper did. The damage is real: whipsawed factories hire and fire, and inventory
    piles up at exactly the wrong tiers.
  </p>
  <p>
    The causes are systematic: order batching, long lead times, price promotions that encourage
    forward-buying, and each tier forecasting from the tier below it instead of from true demand.
    The fixes are informational — share point-of-sale data upstream (the essence of
    retailer–supplier collaboration), cut batch sizes, smooth prices, shorten lead times — which
    is why visibility is treated as the discipline's most valuable commodity.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that the bullwhip means customers are unpredictable. Final
  demand is often the steadiest thing in the chain — it is the <em>ordering behaviour</em> of
  intermediate firms, each protecting itself against uncertainty, that manufactures the wild
  swings. When executives blame "erratic consumer demand" for inventory gluts, the discipline's
  answer is: look upstream at the amplification your own tiers created.</p>

  <h2>SCOR and the metrics that make chains discussable</h2>
  <p>
    A chain that spans companies cannot be managed by goodwill; it needs a shared vocabulary and
    agreed numbers. The <strong>SCOR model</strong> (Supply Chain Operations Reference model,
    stewarded by ASCM, the Association for Supply Chain Management) supplies both. Its process
    vocabulary organises all supply chain work into <strong>Plan</strong> (balance demand and
    supply), <strong>Source</strong> (procure goods and services), <strong>Make</strong>
    (transform), <strong>Deliver</strong> (order management, warehousing, transportation),
    <strong>Return</strong> (reverse logistics) and <strong>Enable</strong> (the rules, data and
    systems the other processes run on; newer SCOR versions add <em>Orchestrate</em> for strategy
    and workforce). The value is precision: "we need to improve logistics" becomes "our Deliver
    process is weak on responsiveness", and two companies that have never worked together can
    still audit each other in the same terms.
  </p>
  <p>
    SCOR's metrics cluster into five attribute families. <strong>Reliability</strong> is measured
    by the <em>perfect order</em> — delivered complete, on time, undamaged, with correct
    documentation — and its rate is the single most customer-visible number in the field, because
    a 97 per cent score means three orders in a hundred failed at somebody's expense.
    <strong>Responsiveness</strong> is the order fulfilment cycle time: order placed to delivery.
    <strong>Agility</strong> measures how fast the chain can scale up for an unexpected surge and
    how long it can sustain a downturn. <strong>Cost</strong> is tracked as total supply chain
    management cost and cost of goods sold. <strong>Asset management efficiency</strong> asks how
    hard the chain's capital works, and its two headline ratios deserve unpacking.
    <em>Inventory turns</em> — cost of goods sold divided by average inventory — compresses the
    whole inventory-versus-availability tension into one comparable figure: a grocer turning stock
    20 times a year holds about 18 days of sales; a heavy-equipment maker turning 2 times holds
    six months. <em>Cash-to-cash cycle time</em> measures the days between paying suppliers and
    being paid by customers — inventory days plus receivable days minus payable days — the number
    that reveals whether growth consumes cash or releases it. In 2020, the firms that weathered
    disruption best were not those with the lowest cash-to-cash but those that could see and flex
    fastest — which is why agility metrics joined the classic efficiency ones.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked example ties the metrics together. Two distributors both sell 10 million units a
    year. Distributor A holds 40 million in average inventory (turns of 2.5, assuming cost basis)
    and a cash-to-cash of 60 days; Distributor B, having invested in shared forecasts with its key
    supplier, holds 16 million (turns of 6) with cash-to-cash of 25 days — at a slightly higher
    transport cost from more frequent, smaller deliveries. B's balance sheet can grow twice as
    fast on the same capital. The chain did not get cheaper; it got <em>faster</em>, and in supply
    chains speed is money.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "supply chain management is logistics — trucks and
  warehouses." Logistics is one SCOR process (Deliver). The discipline also designs networks,
  chooses suppliers, sets inventory policy, forecasts demand and coordinates information across
  companies — the parts that decide whether the trucks have anything to carry and anywhere to
  go. That is why a great logistics manager is not automatically a great supply chain manager:
  the second job is mostly negotiation and design.</p>

  <h2>Customer and supplier segments: no chain is one-size-fits-all</h2>
  <p>
    Because service is expensive, mature supply chains do not serve everyone identically — they
    <strong>segment</strong>. Customer segmentation groups customers by what service they need and
    what they are worth, so scarce capacity goes where it pays. The common shape: customers ranked
    by profitability and volume (the classic ABC analysis), then assigned differentiated promises —
    key accounts get dedicated capacity, priority allocation in shortages and custom delivery
    windows; the mid-tier gets standard service; tiny, high-cost-to-serve accounts may be moved to
    less expensive channels. The analytical work is honest cost-to-serve accounting: a small
    account that orders erratically, demands split shipments and pays in 90 days can consume far
    more chain resources than its margin funds — a fact invisible until the chain is segmented.
  </p>
  <p>
    Supplier segmentation works from the buyer's side, and its standard tool is the
    <strong>Kraljic matrix</strong> (Peter Kraljic, 1983), which plots each purchased item by
    profit impact and supply risk. Four quadrants, four strategies. <em>Strategic</em> items (high
    impact, high risk — the custom chips in a car maker's bill of materials) get partnership:
    joint development, long-term contracts, executive attention. <em>Leverage</em> items (high
    impact, low risk — commodity packaging) get competition: market testing, aggressive
    negotiation, multiple bidders. <em>Bottleneck</em> items (low spend, high risk — a sole-source
    specialty chemical) get risk management: buffer stock, qualified alternates, contract
    security. <em>Non-critical</em> items (office supplies) get efficiency: catalogues,
    automation, and as little managerial attention as possible. The matrix's enduring insight is
    that procurement effort is itself a scarce resource and should be spent where the
    risk-and-value profile justifies it — the corporate world is full of teams negotiating hard
    over leverage items while strategic suppliers drift toward failure unwatched.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the cheapest supplier wins." Price is one column of the
  Kraljic matrix, not the whole of it. A cheap supplier of a bottleneck item that fails in a
  shortage has cost you a production line; an expensive strategic partner who shares forecasts
  and co-develops parts has bought you the whole trade-off triangle. Segmentation exists precisely
  because the cheapest-option rule is wrong for most of what a company buys.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A customer returns a defective laptop, gets a refund, and the retailer logs the fault for the manufacturer. Which of the three flows did each event move, and in which direction?</li>
    <li>A retailer runs a one-week promotion. Explain, tier by tier, how the factory can end up building a month of extra output the shopper never demanded.</li>
    <li>Distributor A turns inventory 2.5 times a year; Distributor B turns 6. What does that difference mean physically, and what did B probably give up to get it?</li>
    <li>A purchased item is cheap to buy but comes from a single specialised supplier. In which Kraljic quadrant does it sit, and what is the right strategy?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The physical laptop is the reverse product flow (downstream
    customer back upstream); the refund is the money flow (upstream to downstream, reversing its
    usual direction); the fault log is the information flow, which should travel both ways — and
    its absence is how chains fail to learn. (2) The promotion makes sales jump one week, so the
    retailer orders more than the true rate (batching plus a buffer); the wholesaler reads the
    bigger order as a trend and adds its own margin; the factory sees two amplified jumps, reads a
    boom, and schedules extra production — forward-buying moved future demand into this week, and
    every tier forecast from orders rather than from shoppers. (3) A holds about 4 months of sales
    in stock; B holds about 2 months. B's capital is free for growth, but B pays slightly more
    transport for frequent, smaller deliveries — the triangle in action: inventory and service
    bought with cost. (4) Bottleneck — low spend, high supply risk. Not negotiation (there is
    nothing to leverage) but risk management: buffer stock, qualified alternates, contract
    security.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A supply chain is every company between raw material and customer — coordinated without being owned; flows, not boxes, are what you manage.</li>
    <li>Product flows downstream (with a reverse flow for returns), money flows upstream, information flows both ways; most failures are information failures.</li>
    <li>Final demand is the only true signal; everything upstream is derived from it — and hides it tier by tier.</li>
    <li>The bullwhip effect: each tier's self-protective ordering amplifies demand variability upstream; the fixes are visibility, small batches, smooth prices, short lead times.</li>
    <li>The trade-off triangle — service, inventory, cost: pick two. Match chain type to product type (efficient for functional, responsive for innovative).</li>
    <li>SCOR's vocabulary (Plan, Source, Make, Deliver, Return, Enable) and five metric families make chains discussable; perfect order, inventory turns and cash-to-cash are the headline numbers.</li>
    <li>Segment customers by worth-and-needs, and suppliers by profit impact and supply risk (Kraljic) — effort is scarce, spend it where it pays.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    You now hold the concepts and the numbers. The next lessons build the machinery on them:
    demand planning and forecasting (where the signals that fight the bullwhip come from),
    inventory management (EOQ, reorder policies, safety stock — the mathematics inside the
    triangle), then procurement and sourcing, production and lean operations, logistics networks,
    global supply chains, and finally risk, resilience and the digital supply chain — the frontier
    this lesson's 2020 examples pointed at.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three refinements carry you toward intermediate work. First, <strong>decoupling
    points</strong>: every chain must choose where to hold its inventory of generic product and
    where final customer demand "enters" the process — make-to-stock (push finished goods to a
    warehouse, as with groceries), assemble-to-order (hold modules, configure at the last step, as
    with laptops), or make-to-order (begin production on the order itself, as with aircraft). The
    postponement principle — keep product generic as long as possible, differentiate late —
    explains why a global apparel brand ships undyed fabric and colours it after early sales data
    arrives. Second, <strong>inventory has types</strong> with different economics: cycle stock
    (the portion that turns over with batch sizes), safety stock (the buffer against forecast
    error and lead-time variability, sized statistically against a target service level), pipeline
    stock (in transit — global commerce holds weeks of sales on ships), and anticipation stock
    (built ahead of predictable seasonal peaks).
  </p>
  <p>
    Third, the strategic question underneath the design: <strong>which links do you own?</strong>
    <em>Vertical integration</em> means owning more than one portion of your chain — backwards
    into suppliers or forwards into distribution. Henry Ford's River Rouge complex was the
    monument to it: by the 1920s Ford owned rubber plantations in Brazil, coal mines in Kentucky,
    iron-ore mines in Michigan and Minnesota, timberland, a shipping fleet and a railroad, all to
    guarantee that the assembly line never stopped. <em>Netflix</em> is the modern backwards case:
    a distribution platform that began producing its own content when licensing partners became
    rivals. <em>Horizontal integration</em> — merging with firms at the same stage — is the other
    direction: Kraft's merger with Heinz in 2015, or Marriott buying Starwood in 2016. Integration
    buys control and coordination at the price of capital and flexibility, which is the trade-off
    triangle again, worn as ownership.
  </p>
  <p>
    And the current research frontier: <strong>resilience economics</strong>. Decades of
    optimisation pushed chains toward lowest cost and just-in-time inventory, and the pandemic,
    the Suez blockage and tariff shocks revealed that resilience was an unpriced insurance policy.
    The field now quantifies the trade explicitly — dual sourcing, nearshoring, and strategic
    stock valued like an option against disruption — which is, in the end, the trade-off triangle
    wearing a new face: you still choose two of service, cost and inventory; resilience is the
    price of keeping the promise under stress.
  </p>

